<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:actipro="http://schemas.actiprosoftware.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:utils="clr-namespace:VDF.GUI.Utils;assembly=VDF.GUI">

  <!-- ===== The dialogs =====
       Every window that is not MainWindow: the message box, the input box, About, the
       blacklist manager, the custom selection, the database viewer, the thumbnail
       comparer, the relocate/expression/quality dialogs.

       They used to be a tinted page with a card floating inside it, under the system
       caption. That is two surfaces and a titlebar where the reference design has one
       surface and none, so the dialogs now take the shape the user pointed at:

         * the caption is gone (no window keeps its system caption, set in XAML because
           MainWindow must keep its own extended caption), so the dialog starts at its
           content instead of a strip carrying the app name;
         * the window itself IS the card - its background is VdfCardBrush and the body
           carries nothing but padding, so there is no nested frame and no second
           colour behind it;
         * the affirmative button is the palette's solid accent, not the soft tint: on
           a card-coloured surface a soft fill on the primary action reads as disabled,
           and the reference's primary is the loudest thing in the box.

       All twelve are WindowDecorations="None", and none extends its client area over a
       frame. BorderOnly is still a non-client frame, and Windows 11 draws that frame on
       the window rectangle together with a drop shadow of its own - while the card is
       inset 28px to give the shadow it paints itself somewhere to land, so the system's
       ring landed 28 to 30px outside the card and read as a second, translucent border
       around the dialog. Extending the client area over the frame is what kept it coming:
       it hid the frame, but the ring follows the window rather than the frame, and the
       window is still a rectangle of its own.

       Taking the frame off took the edges with it, and that is where they stayed until the
       app took them back. With no thick frame the OS answers HTCLIENT the whole way round,
       so a frameless window cannot be pulled at all: the pointer is over client area and
       the client area is content. DialogDrag owns the edges now - a press within six pixels
       of the card's border is taken on the way down, before the contents can swallow it,
       and answered by setting Width and Height with the far edge pinned, corners included.
       Not for every window, though: the five that set CanResize="False" keep their edges
       as body, which is what they asked for. The grip is measured on the card and not on
       the window, because the window's own edge is 28px of transparent margin the shadow
       falls into - six pixels measured from there would sit in dead space.
       No frame line reaches the screen from this file either: the 1px line the dialogs
       used to show was its own BorderBrush (VdfDialogFrameBrush) on Border.dlgframe, and
       that is gone. Only MainWindow keeps the full caption. -->

  <Style Selector="Window">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfCardBrush}" />
    <!-- The palette's main ink, hung on the window so every TextBlock under it inherits
         it (the colour scheme names one 主文字 per theme; the theme's default was its own
         near-black). MainWindow matches this selector too - it sets its Background locally,
         not its Foreground, so it picks this up as well. -->
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VdfTextBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13.5" />
    <!-- Dim the shell behind every dialog. It hangs on the Window selector so none of
         the twelve has to carry it and none can be missed, and DialogDim skips
         MainWindow - this selector matches it too, and the shell must not dim itself. -->
    <Setter Property="utils:DialogDim.AutoDim" Value="True" />
    <!-- A captionless window has nothing the OS will drag it by, so the body is the
         handle; and a frameless one has nothing the OS will size it by either, so the
         card's edge is the grip. Both hang on the same Window rule as the dim, so all
         twelve get them and none can be missed; DialogDrag skips MainWindow, which still
         has its own caption and its own frame. -->
    <Setter Property="utils:DialogDrag.Enabled" Value="True" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The dialog body: padding only. It drew a card with a border and a shadow while the
       window behind it was the page tint; with the window wearing the card surface that
       frame would be a line inside its own colour. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.dlgbody">
    <!-- mockup .modal: 24px down either side, 22px above and below (its head is
         22px 24px 14px and its foot 0 24px 22px; one body padding stands in for both).
         It was 22px/18px/20px, which is not a measure the mockup uses. -->
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="24,22,24,22" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The dialog's own edge, and the reason it had none: the window is the card (the Window
       rule above), and a card-coloured rectangle floating over a page that in two themes is
       the SAME colour is not a thing the eye can find. The reference never relied on the
       fill either - its .modal carries a 1px line-2 outline and its large shadow on top of
       the backdrop that cannot exist here (the dialogs are top-level windows, not layers).
       So the outline is the whole of it: one pixel of VdfDialogFrameBrush - the dialogs' own
       colour, three steps deeper than the hairline this used to borrow, because nothing else
       is lifting the edge (see App.xaml) - the card's own corner, and the card's own shadow
       to lift it.

       Where this hangs is not a detail. Avalonia's Window has no BorderBrush,
       BorderThickness, CornerRadius or BoxShadow - setting them on the Window selector in
       this file would compile, run, and quietly do nothing. They go on the content layer's
       root container instead, which is the class the dialogs carry: six of them on the
       Border.dlgbody they already had, five wrapped in one around their root Grid/DockPanel,
       and the comparer, which had an outline of its own at 2px and square corners, now
       carrying this same class as the other eleven.

       The corners are round again, and what they used to collide with is gone. The window
       used to be given a rectangular 1px non-client frame and a rectangular shadow, so a
       rounded outline on the content layer drew a second, rounder edge inside the window's
       own square one and the four corner squares between them showed the window's
       background. That is why the frame went square. The window is transparent now and
       draws its own shadow, so there is no rectangle to disagree with: the corners are
       the mockup's own radius (26, its largest corner token) and there is only one edge. -->
  <!-- The frame carries no outline of its own. It is not that outlines cannot separate two
       surfaces - it is that a 1px line is not what makes something read as floating, and
       while the line was there nobody noticed there was no shadow: it was doing the
       separating all by itself, which is a job it does poorly. What separates them now is
       the backdrop - the shell behind an open dialog is dimmed (VdfScrimBrush, hung from
       the Window rule above by DialogDim) - and the shadow underneath, which is what
       actually lifts the edge.

       Which is why this class needs air around it. A shadow drawn by the content layer fell
       outside the client area, and the client area is where every pixel gets clipped, so it
       never rendered: the dialogs set the window transparent and pad their content by 28px,
       giving the shadow somewhere real to land (see the TransparencyLevelHint on the twelve
       dialog windows). 24 was the first try and it was not enough - screenshots show the
       falloff still being cut off at the window edge instead of fading out, which is a
       shadow that stops in mid-air. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.dlgframe">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource VdfDialogShadow}" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="TextBlock.dlgtitle">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="15" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.dlgtext">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13.5" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.dlghint">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12.5" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="{DynamicResource VdfTextMutedOpacity}" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>
  <!-- A heading inside a dialog body (the section names in the blacklist manager). -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.dlgsection">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.6" />
    <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="{DynamicResource VdfTextMutedOpacity}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The affirmative action: a solid fill of the palette's accent INK (the deep blue the
       accent text uses) with the on-accent ink on top - white on #17587F in the light theme,
       #0B1F2E on #CDE9FF in the dark one. It was the soft accent fill, which on the card
       surface the window now wears had nothing to stand against; a solid one has to pick a
       fill that can carry its own label. The accent ramp's #4A97CD (the rail badge) cannot:
       white on it is 3.2:1, and the contrast tests in VDF.GUI.HeadlessTests say so.
       The fill and the ink both have to be re-applied under the pointer and while
       pressed, because the theme paints its own colours onto the presenter - the same
       reason Button.solid exists in _Buttons.xaml. This button IS the mockup's
       .btn-primary, exactly as the scan button on the setup page is, so the two share the
       VdfPrimarySolid keys and all three of their steps. The pointer fill is a step of its
       own rather than an opacity fade: fading the whole button fades its label with it. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgprimary">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfPrimarySolidBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VdfOnPrimarySolidBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="9" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="18,7" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="96" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgprimary:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfPrimarySolidHoverBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- BorderBrush is pinned along with the fill and the ink, and for the same reason: the
       mockup's .btn-primary rests on a 1px transparent frame, but the theme's pointerover
       rule paints its own grey onto the presenter, so hovering the affirmative button drew
       a grey outline nobody asked for (measured: #9FA3AD on the part in view while the
       button's own frame was still transparent). Pinned to the button, the frame stays
       Transparent in all three states. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgprimary /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{Binding Background, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{Binding BorderBrush, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgprimary:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{Binding Background, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{Binding BorderBrush, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgprimary:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{Binding Background, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{Binding BorderBrush, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The other choices: an outlined button. It was a card surface with the card's own
       hairline, which works on a tinted page and disappears on the card the window now
       is; the outline steps up to the muted border so the safe answer still reads as a
       button. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgghost">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VdfMutedBorderBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="9" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="18,7" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="96" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgghost:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfBtnHoverBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VdfBtnHoverLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgghost:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VdfTextBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A window with no caption has no close button either, so the ones whose only way out
       used to be the caption ✕ (the database viewer, the comparer) carry this one in their
       own header row. "closered" is the red-under-the-pointer half on its own, for a close
       button that is not a bare 28x28 glyph — the comparer's "✕ 关闭对比器", which is wider
       and would be squeezed by this style's Width. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgclose">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="28" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="28" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
    <!-- No ink of its own: VdfMutedBrush on the sunken toolbar is 2.8:1, under the 3:1 a
         glyph needs (caught by DialogContrastTests). The window's foreground follows the
         theme and clears it in all four. -->
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgclose /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
  </Style>
  <!-- Under the pointer the ✕ turns into the red every other "this is how you leave" control
       in the app speaks: the caption is gone, so the button is the only way out and a sunken
       grey stated nothing. The ink goes white on the red (5.2:1, which the contrast suite
       measures on the pressed state as well). -->
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgclose:pointerover, Button.closered:pointerover">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VdfOnCloseHoverBrush}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Button.dlgclose:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter, Button.closered:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfCloseHoverBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VdfOnCloseHoverBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Text fields, lists and tables inside a dialog take the sunken surface: the theme
       gives them the chrome grey, which is the one thing that still looked foreign. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBox.dlgfield">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfSunkenBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VdfCardLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="9" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,7" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.dlglist">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfSunkenBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VdfCardLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="12" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4" />
  </Style>
  <!-- The theme answers the pointer with a neutral grey and selection with its own accent;
       both read as a second, unrelated palette inside a dialog. Same tint the pages use.

       Both ways of drawing one have to be named. Every dialog that has drawn a list so far
       put .dlglist on a Border and set a plain ListBox inside it, so a selector written for
       the ListBox alone matched nothing at all - those rows stayed on whatever the theme
       answers the pointer with, which in dark is a #4273A4 carrying our own 13.5px label at
       3.38:1. Two of those dialogs draw one this way, so it is the way to cover.
       The foreground rides along on both: the theme rewrites it to suit the fill it chose,
       so it is pinned back to the row's own with $parent, the same binding the results list
       uses for its selected row. Without it the label changes colour under the pointer and
       not by our hand. -->
  <Style Selector="ListBox.dlglist ListBoxItem:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter,
                   Border.dlglist ListBoxItem:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfRowHoverBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding $parent[ListBoxItem].Foreground}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ListBox.dlglist ListBoxItem:selected /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter,
                   Border.dlglist ListBoxItem:selected /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VdfRowSelectedBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{Binding $parent[ListBoxItem].Foreground}" />
  </Style>
</Styles>
